WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:10.966
My name is Ed Fischer, and I'm a retired professor of biochemistry

00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:15.132
at the University of Washington. Today I would like to give you

00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:20.799
a sort of rapid historical overview of the discovery of reversible

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.799
protein phosphorylation as a regulatory mechanism.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.566
Now this came about in the course of a study that

00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:34.999
we had undertaken with my colleague and longtime friend,

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:42.366
Ed Krebs, on glycogen phosphorylase 60 years ago. The

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:46.899
original reaction we described was really embarrassingly

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.832
simple. And nobody would have paid much attention to it

00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:56.066
if it didn't turn out to be absolutely crucial for the regulation

00:00:56.067 --> 00:01:00.866
of cellular processes. On the other hand, it's fair to say that when we

00:01:00.867 --> 00:01:05.732
came up with it, it came as a total surprise because in those days

00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:11.499
one knew essentially nothing about cell and enzyme regulation.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:17.799
The prevailing idea was that enzymes were regulated

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:23.399
by the rate at which they were synthesized and then degraded.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:28.466
And that stood until the late '40s and early '50s, when it was

00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:33.332
realized that this couldn't be the case. That cells had to have

00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:38.399
ways of manipulating the activity of their enzymes once they

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.932
had been produced and liberated inside the cell. And that

00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:49.599
was really one of the big problems that faced the biologists in those

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.499
days and that was the problem that we decided to tackle

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:59.999
with Ed. We wanted to know how the activity of an important

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.266
enzyme called glycogen phosphorylase was regulated.

00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:10.899
It was very important because it was already known to catalyze

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:16.199
the rate limiting step of carbohydrate metabolism. The first step in the

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:21.266
degradation of glycogen. And furthermore, it was under complex

00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:27.832
hormonal regulation, being activated by adrenaline and

00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:35.732
inhibited by insulin. When first discovered in the early '30s,

00:02:35.733 --> 00:02:42.232
by Yakov Parnas in Poland and Carl and Gerty Cori in the United States,

00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:48.166
the muscle enzyme was known to be totally inactive in the absence

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:54.966
of AMP, adenylic acid. And automatically, the Coris assumed that

00:02:54.967 --> 00:03:02.366
AMP served as a coenzyme, and that stood until about '43,

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:09.032
when Arda Green and Coris crystallized the native form

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:17.732
of the active form of the enzyme. But she crystallized it in a new form.

00:03:17.733 --> 00:03:26.799
In that it was fully active in the absence of AMP. So they called that

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:33.866
form phosphorylase a and logically assumed that it had to contain

00:03:33.867 --> 00:03:39.299
AMP bound covalently to the protein as a prosthetic group.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:45.299
And furthermore, they assumed that that had to be the native

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:49.866
form of the enzyme in muscle, because when muscle extracts

00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:55.366
were left standing, it was rapidly converted to the old form

00:03:55.367 --> 00:04:00.832
that they now call phosphorylase b, the form that required AMP

00:04:00.833 --> 00:04:08.332
for activity. But they could show no formation of AMP in the reaction

00:04:08.333 --> 00:04:13.699
and furthermore, they could show no AMP in the native

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:20.499
enzyme. So they were stuck. The Coris knew that phosphorylase

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:27.532
existed in two forms, active and inactive, but they did not

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:33.732
know in which way those two forms differed. And as strange as it would

00:04:33.733 --> 00:04:40.866
seem today, they actually dropped that problem. Ed and I became

00:04:40.867 --> 00:04:47.532
involved in that problem when we found ourselves in the same department

00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:54.166
in Seattle. Ed had been a postdoc of the Coris and had some

00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:59.966
hesitations to work on that problem. In those days, it should be said

00:04:59.967 --> 00:05:04.266
that students and postdocs when they left the lab did not

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:10.466
work on the problem of their mentors. But then he reasoned that he had left

00:05:10.467 --> 00:05:15.832
St. Louis five years ago and it should be permissible for us to work

00:05:15.833 --> 00:05:21.932
together on that problem. Okay, so we cleared the bench in the lab and started

00:05:21.933 --> 00:05:26.866
working, just the two of us side by side, much more like two friends than

00:05:26.867 --> 00:05:31.932
like two colleagues in the department. And the first thing we had to do

00:05:31.933 --> 00:05:37.832
of course was to purify phosphorylase, get the pure enzyme,

00:05:37.833 --> 00:05:43.866
by Coris' classical procedure. Now Coris' procedure consisted in

00:05:43.867 --> 00:05:50.366
taking rabbit muscle, grinding it up, extracting the hamburger in

00:05:50.367 --> 00:05:55.266
water, throwing the whole thing in cheesecloth, squeezing

00:05:55.267 --> 00:05:59.899
the cheese cloth, and then clarifying the very turbid

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.999
extract by passing it through a battery of filter papers.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:09.032
And of course the filter paper became clogged up right away,

00:06:09.033 --> 00:06:12.799
so with a glass rod, they would punch a hole and go to the next

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:20.232
paper, and then the next one. It wasn't unusual for them to use

00:06:20.233 --> 00:06:26.832
maybe 15-20 filter papers for one purification. The method

00:06:26.833 --> 00:06:33.699
was totally archaic and in fact, here's a picture of our lab.

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:40.432
So, we decided with Ed, enough with that jazz. Let's replace

00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:46.066
paper filtration by a centrifugation. In defense of the Coris,

00:06:46.067 --> 00:06:49.332
it should be said that in those days there was no good

00:06:49.333 --> 00:06:54.232
lab centrifuge. But then, when we centrifuged our extract,

00:06:54.233 --> 00:06:59.932
we could never obtain active phosphorylase a, only the

00:06:59.933 --> 00:07:05.566
so-called "degraded b form." Until finally, in desperation,

00:07:05.567 --> 00:07:09.732
we said, you know we have to go back to Coris' procedure.

00:07:09.733 --> 00:07:14.566
Following it to the letter, paper filtration, and all. Measuring

00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:20.366
every step to see what's going on. And to our immense

00:07:20.367 --> 00:07:26.432
surprise, this is what we found. That the very first extract from

00:07:26.433 --> 00:07:29.499
the muscle, which should have contained phosphorylase a,

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:34.932
contained the inactive form of the enzyme. And then when it passed

00:07:34.933 --> 00:07:39.799
through the filter paper step, it was converted to active

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:48.032
phosphorylase a. Speaking of a let down would be to speak

00:07:48.033 --> 00:07:53.399
it easily, because paper filtration as a method to activate an enzyme

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:59.866
was really the pits. Pretty below the worst. But, it soon happened

00:07:59.867 --> 00:08:04.166
that it was not the filtration perse that did the trick, but

00:08:04.167 --> 00:08:09.299
the fact that all filter papers in those days were contaminated

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:16.532
by calcium ions. And enough was picked up during those multiple

00:08:16.533 --> 00:08:22.266
filtrations to convert inactive to active phosphorylase. And then

00:08:22.267 --> 00:08:29.566
quite rapidly, we found that ATP was always present in crude muscle

00:08:29.567 --> 00:08:33.632
extract was also needed. And that strongly suggested that we

00:08:33.633 --> 00:08:39.099
were dealing with a phosphorylation reaction. Of course we didn't know what

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:42.299
had become phosphorylated, because in those days you had

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:49.099
no ATP 32, you had to synthesize the damn thing yourself.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:54.366
But we knew that Art Kornberg in St. Louis had made some for his

00:08:54.367 --> 00:08:58.766
beautiful studies of DNA synthesis. So we called Art and he

00:08:58.767 --> 00:09:03.632
right away sent us a sample of gamma-labeled ATP 32,

00:09:03.633 --> 00:09:08.499
with which we could show that radioactivity was incorporated

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:14.232
in a protein fraction, which we could isolate and which turned out

00:09:14.233 --> 00:09:19.466
to be phosphorylase. So then for the first time, we could then

00:09:19.467 --> 00:09:24.499
suggest that the activation of phosphorylase occurred like so.

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:31.732
That the inactive enzyme was converted, was phosphorylated,

00:09:31.733 --> 00:09:37.499
converted to the active form in a reaction that required calcium,

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:42.166
magnesium, and ATP, and an enzyme of course, which we called

00:09:42.167 --> 00:09:47.666
phosphorylase kinase. And then the reverse reaction happened to be

00:09:47.667 --> 00:09:53.699
catalyzed by a phosphorylase phosphatase. And immediately

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:59.132
we shipped the paper to the Journal of Biological Chemistry.

00:09:59.133 --> 00:10:07.266
And we knew that Carl Cori was one of the editors, so you know,

00:10:07.267 --> 00:10:12.432
perhaps a question, but then we had faith that Cori would not

00:10:12.433 --> 00:10:17.866
take advantage of that situation. And he did not, in fact he was very

00:10:17.867 --> 00:10:23.132
excited by our results and he never touched that problem.

00:10:23.133 --> 00:10:31.299
Now, the reaction that I showed earlier became slightly more complicated

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:38.632
when we began to use partially purified elements. Particularly

00:10:38.633 --> 00:10:43.566
when we used partially purified phosphorylase kinase.

00:10:43.567 --> 00:10:50.532
Because it became clear that calcium could not act in

00:10:50.533 --> 00:10:56.632
that reaction. And yet, it was absolutely needed when crude

00:10:56.633 --> 00:11:01.432
muscle extracts were used. So the only possibility is

00:11:01.433 --> 00:11:07.066
that in crude muscle extract, calcium had to act at an earlier

00:11:07.067 --> 00:11:13.299
step. And we hypothesized that perhaps phosphorylase kinase

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:20.032
just like phosphorylase, might also exist in an inactive and

00:11:20.033 --> 00:11:24.732
active form. And perhaps calcium was required for that reaction,

00:11:24.733 --> 00:11:30.499
and that turned out to be correct. In the presence of calcium

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:36.299
ions, phosph kinase is rapidly phosphorylated and activated.

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:43.699
At that same time, Earl Sutherland, Ted Rall, and his group

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:49.066
had isolated cyclic AMP, which they showed had served as

00:11:49.067 --> 00:11:53.632
a second messenger, intracellular messenger, for the action of

00:11:53.633 --> 00:11:58.666
adrenaline. So Earl immediately sent us a sample of his

00:11:58.667 --> 00:12:04.966
material, and we could show that it acted on the activation

00:12:04.967 --> 00:12:12.332
of phosph kinase. Either by accelerating the rate of autophosphorylation

00:12:12.333 --> 00:12:20.066
or perhaps even, by acting on yet another enzyme, which for want of a better

00:12:20.067 --> 00:12:26.499
term, we called a kinase kinase. Some people thought it was a funny type

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:30.866
of name, others thought it was a dumb name. And we never forget the

00:12:30.867 --> 00:12:36.599
comments of the reviewer who looked at this paper, first of all, he

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:40.799
didn't believe that another enzyme would be involved. He said,

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:46.366
when will it end? But then we said, if really another enzyme

00:12:46.367 --> 00:12:51.899
is involved, I wish that the authors would think of a better name than

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:58.866
a kinase kinase. Anyway, that hypothesis was confirmed 4 years

00:12:58.867 --> 00:13:04.866
later by the isolation, by Ed and Don Walsh, of the cyclic AMP

00:13:04.867 --> 00:13:10.599
dependent protein kinase. And since by that time, Earl and his

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:16.032
group had clarified the production of cyclic AMP at the membrane,

00:13:16.033 --> 00:13:23.366
the whole cascade for the phosphorolysis of glycogen was

00:13:23.367 --> 00:13:30.099
elucidated. We have been often asked by Ed whether we realized

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:34.366
at the beginning that we were dealing with a very ubiquitous

00:13:34.367 --> 00:13:39.966
and therefore very fundamental reaction. Absolutely not.

00:13:39.967 --> 00:13:43.432
We knew that it was a nice reaction, we knew that it was an

00:13:43.433 --> 00:13:48.332
important and exciting one, we stayed with it, but we could

00:13:48.333 --> 00:13:52.132
never have guessed the incredible developments that followed.

00:13:52.133 --> 00:13:59.599
Should be said that 4-5 years later, everybody was thinking in terms of

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:04.832
allostery. Jacques Monod, Jeffries Wyman, and Jean-Pierre Changeux

00:14:04.833 --> 00:14:09.932
had come up with a model of allosteric activation of enzymes.

00:14:09.933 --> 00:14:13.499
And it was very clear to me that Jacque Monod was a very

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:19.099
close friend of mine, never believed one second that covalent

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:24.399
modification by protein phosphorylation could serve a fundamental

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:30.499
role in the regulation of cellular processes. We know today

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:37.066
that phosphorylation is one of the most prevalent mechanisms of regulation,

00:14:37.067 --> 00:14:45.999
and it is clear that it would be very difficult to find a physiological

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:52.599
reaction that was not directly or indirectly affected by protein

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:58.366
phosphorylation. Another question that was often asked

00:14:58.367 --> 00:15:03.766
was how was it for you guys to work for more than 50 years

00:15:03.767 --> 00:15:11.432
together? Well, it was very easy and most enjoyable.

00:15:11.433 --> 00:15:18.666
At first, Ed would make fun, would laugh at my English. Every morning,

00:15:18.667 --> 00:15:22.166
I would say, "let's go take a coffee." Because in French we say,

00:15:22.167 --> 00:15:26.599
prendre un cafe. And he really made fun of that.

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:31.599
What Ed didn't realize was that in the following year,

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:36.232
while my English didn't improve very much, his deteriorated

00:15:36.233 --> 00:15:41.532
completely. Anyway, those were marvelous, marvelous days.

00:15:41.533 --> 00:15:47.532
And during all the time we worked together, we never considered

00:15:47.533 --> 00:15:52.532
what we were doing as work. It was never work.

00:15:52.533 --> 00:16:19.767
It was fun. Thank you.

